  "problem": "Sam has $\\frac{1}{3}$ cubic meters of wooden boards to make $2$ dog houses. If he uses the same amount of boards for each dog house, how many cubic meters does Sam use for each dog house?\n\nExpressed as a fraction, Sam uses\n{{field-1}} cubic meters of boards for each dog house.",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the amount of boards used for each dog house, we divide the total amount of boards by the number of dog houses.\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷2\n$$\n\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷2=\\frac{1}{3\\times 2}=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sam spent $\\frac{1}{6}$ cubic meters of boards for each dog house.",
